{
  "image_name": "2cee6a271a9975bcd14c45100af24f4f.png",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes how a platelet becomes activated after contacting exposed collagen and immunoglobulin at a damaged vessel surface. At the membrane, the receptor GPVI with FcRγ binds collagen, triggering ITAM-dependent signaling and activation of tyrosine kinases. This leads to platelet activation together with a rise in intracellular calcium, shown near the dense tubular network, and promotes firm platelet adhesion.\n\nActivated platelets then release several classes of mediators. Small microparticles bud off from the platelet, while contents from α-granules are secreted. P-selectin, initially stored inside the platelet, is moved or released to the surface/cytoskeleton. The figure also highlights soluble inflammatory signals, including CD40L, CCL2, and CCL5, being exported outward.\n\nThe overall message is that platelet stimulation by collagen does more than produce adhesion: it initiates a broader inflammatory and communication program. Arrows from released microparticles, granule products, and P-selectin point to downstream effects such as CRP activation, intercellular crosstalk, and chemotaxis. In other words, the platelet is depicted as both a hemostatic cell that binds firmly at injury sites and an immune-like signaling hub that recruits and influences other cells.",
